"Open an American cookbook and you will not find many grams. You will find cups, tablespoons, and teaspoons. “Add 1 cup of flour and 2 tablespoons of oil,” the recipe says. In Taiwan, a recipe is more likely to give grams and milliliters. Neither way is wrong. They are two different answers to the same question: how much?": "b81c3543d87c",
"Open an American cookbook and you will not find many grams. You will find cups, tablespoons, and teaspoons. “Add 1 cup of flour and 2 tablespoons of oil,” the recipe says. In Taiwan, a recipe is more likely to give grams and milliliters. Neither way is wrong. They are two different answers to the same question: how much? The first way measures volume, which is how much space something takes up. A cup, a tablespoon, and a teaspoon are all units of volume. In the United States, one cup is about 237 milliliters. Three teaspoons make one tablespoon, and sixteen tablespoons make one cup. In the kitchen, people round a teaspoon to 5 milliliters and a tablespoon to 15. The second way measures weight, which is how heavy something is. A kitchen scale shows it in grams. Here is the surprise. Fill the same cup with water, then with flour, then with sugar, and weigh it each time. The cup is full every time, but the scale shows about 237 grams of water, about 125 grams of flour, and about 200 grams of sugar. Why are they different? Flour is a fine powder with a lot of air between its tiny pieces, so a cup of it is light. Sugar crystals pack closer together. Even the same flour can change. Spoon it in loosely, and a cup is about 125 grams. Pack it down, and the same cup can hold up to about 160 grams. This is why many bakers weigh their flour. Recipe English has its own short forms. The letters T S P mean teaspoon, and T B S P means tablespoon. People read one half cup as “half a cup,” and one and one half cups as “one and a half cups.” “A pinch of salt” is the tiny amount you can pick up between your thumb and one finger. The sign, a small circle and the letter F, means degrees Fahrenheit, so an oven at 350 degrees Fahrenheit is at about 177 degrees Celsius. So how do you change a recipe? For water and other thin liquids, multiply the cups by about 237 to get milliliters. For dry things, you must know what is in the cup, because each food has its own number of grams per cup. Remember, too, that a cup is not the same size everywhere. A cup in Australia is 250 milliliters, and the cup that comes with a rice cooker is only about 180. Before you cook, ask one question: whose cup is this?": "9f1cb7099788",
